NAME
    iso-boot-report.sh - inspect boot entries inside MBOOTDATA ISO files

SYNOPSIS
    ./tests/iso-boot-report.sh --data-root DIRECTORY

DESCRIPTION
    Reads each *.iso in DIRECTORY/m/boot/iso using xorriso and reports
    the volume ID, El Torito boot metadata and boot-related internal
    file paths, including GRUB configurations, EFI fallback programs,
    kernel/initrd paths and Windows PE boot material when present.
    Checks native Memtest86+ 9.00 files under
    DIRECTORY/m/boot/efi/memtest using file.

    All ISO data and mounted filesystems are READ ONLY. No mounting,
    flashing, copying or opening block devices. Uses a private temporary
    directory for intermediate inventories and deletes it on exit.
    stdout is the report, stderr is for failures.

REQUIREMENTS
    Linux host with xorriso, file, mktemp and a readable mounted
    MBOOTDATA filesystem. The mounted ext4 filesystem should not be
    exposed to a QEMU raw disk guest simultaneously with this utility.

EXAMPLE
    sudo apt install -y xorriso file
    ./tests/iso-boot-report.sh \
      --data-root /run/media/admino/MBOOTDATA \
      > "$HOME/mboot-iso-boot-report.txt"

INTERPRETATION
    The El Torito BIOS/UEFI boot catalog shows whether an ISO exposes
    optical boot facilities; it does not make a raw ISO file act as a CD.
    Presence of boot/grub/loopback.cfg indicates a candidate GRUB file
    boot path. A generic boot/grub/grub.cfg may assume physical media.
    Kernel/initrd file locations are evidence for researching explicit
    boot options, not automatically a valid recipe.
    HBCD PE uses Windows PE artifacts rather than a Linux live root.
    An ISO file may boot from QEMU virtual CD and fail through GRUB
    loopback on the physical MBOOTDATA filesystem. Such differential
    evidence is important for selecting a boot bridge mechanism.

    This is static inspection, not a QEMU/firmware/OS boot result.
    Record genuine visual start/menu/kernel/live-root outcomes separately.

    When a specific ISO fails in m-loader, an optional differential
    test is to present that exact file directly as a QEMU virtual optical
    drive, WITHOUT attaching /dev/sda or any other physical whole disk.
    Example on a Linux VM with the data filesystem normally mounted:

        iso=/run/media/admino/MBOOTDATA/m/boot/iso/proxmox-ve_9.2-1.iso
        qemu-system-x86_64 -machine pc -accel tcg -m 3072 -smp 2 \
          -boot d -cdrom "$iso" -snapshot

    The ISO is read by QEMU as an optical disc; no data-partition raw
    disk is exposed to the guest and no physical USB writes are needed.
    Observe optical firmware menu/kernel/installer stages, not just
    process exit. On a small host, slower emulation or insufficient RAM
    can prevent full installer validation. Do not use this baseline
    to claim that GRUB loopback works.

DIAGNOSTICS
    Nonzero exit means missing operands/prerequisites, no ISO images
    or one or more unreadable ISO inventories. Diagnostics identify
    the failing file; successful images are still reported.

